= Strengths =

* Highest possible melee damage in the game, most likely.
* Despite being pure melee, has a splattering of useful utility
* Gear versatility lets you wear pretty much whatever gives the melee stats you need and AC will stay high. 

= Weaknesses =

* Utility is going to get trumped by any real utility
* Poor HP Management tools
* Endurance struggles without proper downtime and short enough fights

= Core Strategy =

Ultimately, your strategy is simple: Toggle auto attack and auto skill on, try to switch into Striker if you know you won't need to stretch your endurance. If you run into something particularly challenging, you can FD in between active-skill combos and "FD Joust" the mob, avoiding virtually all auto attacks in between your rounds of burst.

== Pulling ==

FD presents a challenge on this server without a duo partner, but it's still your best tool. The classic tricks of snare, root, mez, etc work well enough to split mobs, but the "forget" mechanics on this server do make it painfully slow to get the mob you want out of a stack. Take advantage of pulling back to corners or other places where you can take advantage of Line of Sight. When something starts going back home, unfeign and refeign immediately to potentially force the threat list window to reset. This window is your only way to have a clue what's going on with mob resets.

== Tanking ==

Unfortunately, the ways in which this class minimizes incoming damage is offset by the strongest tank tool sharing the only BER/MNK/ROG resource pool. You don't take a lot of damage, but if you're wiping out your endurance, you won't have any left for Evasive. If you lean on evasive, you won't have any for your damage stances. Even then, you're going to find yourself switching between either offensive or berserker until you're at around 50% hp and mend is on cooldown, and evasive to buy time for mend to come back up. If you run out of endurance, you should be switching into balanced and switching between balanced and evasive. Outside of evasive, you're killable.

== Healing ==

You get heals over time from Blood Rune and from Lifetap Poison. Both of which require being somewhat injured, so starting out in evasive isn't super helpful. Your best bet to take full advantage is to burst until you're at half health, mend to 75-90, and then lifetap poison/blood rune yourself stable until mend is off cooldown, burn the mob, repeat. Mend is your savior and you should get it on cooldown ASAP. Your other heals will total ~20 heals per second at best at the top end. That's currently not enough to sustain, so every fight is a race between your HP/Endurance and their HP.

== Crowd Control ==

lol. You get an interrupt and a snare with berserker, and some stun proc in rogue, but no hard CC is a big weakness of this build.

== Damage ==

This is all you do. Don't toggle auto attack on too soon or your active abilities will all swing at the air and be wasted for the first round. 

== Buffs and Debuffs ==

Rely on clickies and gear for this. Rogue poisons as seen below are a great tool and you can run multiple of them. Slow, Stun, Mana Drain, Snare, Cast Speed Reduction, Interrupt and a pushback from a junk Monk AA are all you have.

== Emergency Tools ==

Managing your threat in a duo is kind of a breeze. Escape AA is very powerful for this, on a long cooldown, with hide and FD making up the bulk of your in-fight "get it off me" mechanics. 

= Leveling Strategy =

Overall, you're just going to be slow killing one mob at a time. A duo with a shaman would be great for speeding things up, but it washes out if they're not doing almost as much DPS as you do.

